  "title": "Ascending aorta function effector gene predictions (Benjamins et al., 2022)",
  "bodyHtml": "<p>This table displays results from the study:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Genomic insights in ascending aortic size and distensibility.<br />\r\nBenjamins JW, <i>et al.</i><br />\r\nEBioMedicine. 2022 Jan;75:103783. doi: 10.1016/j.ebiom.2021.103783.<br />\r\nPMID: <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/34968759\" target=\"_blank\"> 34968759</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The list below displays candidate causal genes for properties of the ascending aorta:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n\t<li>Ascending aorta maximum area (AAo max)</li>\r\n\t<li>Ascending aorta minimum area (AAo min)</li>\r\n\t<li>Ascending aorta distensibility (AAo dist)</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Genes were prioritized at loci genetically associated with the aortic properties listed above, based on three evidence types: results from DEPICT (Data-driven Expression Prioritized Integration for Complex Traits; <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25597830/\" target=\"_blank\">Pers TH <i>et al</i>., 2015</a>); presence of a coding variant in high LD with the lead SNP; and proximity of a gene to the lead SNP (nearest gene). Click the Research Method tab for more details.</p>\r\n",
